WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.234
Welcome to Building Defender XDR,

2
00:00:02.234 --> 00:00:06.212
a developer workbook in the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

3
00:00:06.212 --> 00:00:10.151
This is Chapter 9: deploying
to test and production.

4
00:00:10.151 --> 00:00:14.343
The same commands run in both stages;
only the settings file changes,

5
00:00:14.343 --> 00:00:18.312
and production waits until the
test stage's own evidence says go.

6
00:00:18.312 --> 00:00:22.974
This chapter is a runbook, not a
gate, so it has no labs of its own.

7
00:00:22.974 --> 00:00:26.622
Every step is a command,
and the outputs on these

8
00:00:26.622 --> 00:00:30.363
slides are from fixture
mode on November 17, 2026.

9
00:00:30.363 --> 00:00:33.013
Nothing in it has been
run against a live tenant,

10
00:00:33.013 --> 00:00:36.010
and the chapter has been
reviewed and approved.

11
00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:41.960
Why the same commands in both stages?

12
00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:46.685
Because a runbook that differs between
test and production isn't tested at all.

13
00:00:46.685 --> 00:00:48.541
Only the settings file changes,

14
00:00:48.541 --> 00:00:52.323
and production waits until the
test stage's own evidence says go.

15
00:00:52.323 --> 00:00:55.624
By the end of this chapter you
can take the kit to a test tenant,

16
00:00:55.624 --> 00:00:59.784
prove it there with evidence, and take
it to production under a change record,

17
00:00:59.784 --> 00:01:02.623
with a rollback planned
before anything is applied.

18
00:01:02.623 --> 00:01:06.682
The people who run the change are the
platform owner with the operator, the

19
00:01:06.682 --> 00:01:08.944
detection engineer for the publish step,

20
00:01:08.944 --> 00:01:11.640
and the security officer
for the go or no-go.

21
00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:16.977
Here's the division of labor.

22
00:01:16.977 --> 00:01:21.130
The kit reads through Graph,
compares every input with its plan,

23
00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:26.002
writes the evidence, plans the rollback,
and publishes custom detections,

24
00:01:26.002 --> 00:01:29.915
but only from an approved plan,
with apply and the plan's hash.

25
00:01:29.915 --> 00:01:31.856
A person, under the change record,

26
00:01:31.856 --> 00:01:35.954
assigns roles through Privileged
Identity Management and signs in,

27
00:01:35.954 --> 00:01:40.555
moves an attack surface reduction or
prevention ring in Intune from the kit's

28
00:01:40.555 --> 00:01:42.424
values, approves the plan's hash,

29
00:01:42.424 --> 00:01:46.090
and changes device group levels
and exclusions in the portal.

30
00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:48.165
Only one write is automated.

31
00:01:48.165 --> 00:01:52.412
Learn doesn't state the
detection API's GCC availability,

32
00:01:52.412 --> 00:01:57.570
so if a test-tenant call fails, the
portal path uses the same files.

33
00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.882
Each environment gets one settings file,

34
00:02:03.882 --> 00:02:06.745
copied from the template
onto agency storage.

35
00:02:06.745 --> 00:02:09.989
Fill every placeholder: the
tenant, the output root,

36
00:02:09.989 --> 00:02:14.622
the groups that Privileged Identity
Management governs, the emergency

37
00:02:14.622 --> 00:02:19.333
access groups and accounts, and every
input file the earlier chapters use.

38
00:02:19.333 --> 00:02:21.692
No secret ever goes in a settings file:

39
00:02:21.692 --> 00:02:26.869
the run signs in through Privileged Identity
Management, or as a managed identity.

40
00:02:26.869 --> 00:02:31.020
The cloud is GCC, the Graph
environment is Global.

41
00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:35.922
Every step in this runbook has two forms:
a fixture form with an environment file,

42
00:02:35.922 --> 00:02:40.450
which is what these slides show, and
the live form, which leaves it out.

43
00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:45.225
Who signs what?

44
00:02:45.225 --> 00:02:48.660
The platform owner and the
operator run the pre-flight,

45
00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:52.400
the scheduled run and each ring
change, in the change window.

46
00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.008
The detection engineer and the
approver read the detection plan,

47
00:02:56.008 --> 00:02:59.750
and record its hash in the change
record before anything is applied.

48
00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:04.938
And the security officer signs the go or
no-go, from the test stage's own evidence.

49
00:03:04.938 --> 00:03:09.138
Remember: nothing in this chapter
has been run against a live tenant.

50
00:03:09.138 --> 00:03:12.720
Every live result is
written as an expectation.

51
00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:17.333
Step 1.

52
00:03:17.333 --> 00:03:21.432
Sign in to Graph in the Global
environment, then run the pre-flight.

53
00:03:21.432 --> 00:03:23.308
Six checks for the test stage.

54
00:03:23.308 --> 00:03:26.349
The settings: the
environment is the stage,

55
00:03:26.349 --> 00:03:30.433
every input is named and exists,
and the deploy path is set.

56
00:03:30.433 --> 00:03:32.532
The Graph module is installed.

57
00:03:32.532 --> 00:03:36.746
The session is in the Global environment
and the tenant the settings name.

58
00:03:36.746 --> 00:03:40.557
The granted scopes cover every
read, and the detection write.

59
00:03:40.557 --> 00:03:42.657
A first read of incidents answers.

60
00:03:42.657 --> 00:03:44.858
And the output root is writable.

61
00:03:44.858 --> 00:03:46.858
Any failure stops the stage.

62
00:03:46.858 --> 00:03:51.060
Then record the pre-flight as
evidence, with the change record.

63
00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.260
Step 2 is the first scheduled run.

64
00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:00.944
It runs every check and the metrics
into a folder for the month,

65
00:04:00.944 --> 00:04:05.134
records each one in the stage's
evidence folder with the change record,

66
00:04:05.134 --> 00:04:07.735
and writes a scheduled-run
record of its own.

67
00:04:07.735 --> 00:04:09.833
In fixture mode, most checks fail,

68
00:04:09.833 --> 00:04:13.797
because the fixtures carry each
chapter's deliberate faults.

69
00:04:13.797 --> 00:04:18.728
In a test tenant, each failure is a
list of findings in its run folder:

70
00:04:18.728 --> 00:04:21.686
tickets, plan corrections, role changes.

71
00:04:21.686 --> 00:04:24.360
Close them before the go or no-go.

72
00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:31.873
Step 3 publishes the
detections, dry run first.

73
00:04:31.873 --> 00:04:36.182
Without apply, the script writes what
it would send: a post that creates the

74
00:04:36.182 --> 00:04:40.634
dump-tool rule, and a patch that updates
the encoded PowerShell rule's status.

75
00:04:40.634 --> 00:04:42.310
Then read the plan's hash.

76
00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:45.080
The detection engineer
and the approver read the

77
00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:47.648
plan and record that hash
in the change record.

78
00:04:47.648 --> 00:04:50.997
Only then run it again with
apply and the approved hash.

79
00:04:50.997 --> 00:04:53.948
A plan that changed after
approval is refused.

80
00:04:53.948 --> 00:04:55.935
Record the publish as evidence.

81
00:04:55.935 --> 00:04:58.899
Live, the script pauses
after every ten creates,

82
00:04:58.899 --> 00:05:02.460
which is Learn's quota per
application per minute.

83
00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:08.210
Step 4 is the smoke test.

84
00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:12.896
Run one synthetic event from the guide's
table on a ring 0 device, under the

85
00:05:12.896 --> 00:05:17.660
change record: the replay behind a
newly published rule's known-bad window.

86
00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:21.585
Within the rule's schedule, an alert
from a custom detection appears.

87
00:05:21.585 --> 00:05:24.207
Re-run the detection
check, and the plan should

88
00:05:24.207 --> 00:05:27.173
hold no create or update
for the rules you published.

89
00:05:27.173 --> 00:05:29.860
Step 5 moves one ring, by hand.

90
00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:34.032
The change board takes ring 1's
next value from Chapter 4's run,

91
00:05:34.032 --> 00:05:39.372
the operator puts it in the ring 1 policy
in Intune, and the plan file is updated.

92
00:05:39.372 --> 00:05:42.745
After the devices check in,
run the A S R check again,

93
00:05:42.745 --> 00:05:45.820
and the device checks show the new modes.

94
00:05:49.570 --> 00:05:52.570
Step 6: plan the rollback
before you need it.

95
00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:57.182
The rollback plan disables every detection
rule the stage created or enabled,

96
00:05:57.182 --> 00:05:59.807
which is the guide's
rollback for detections.

97
00:05:59.807 --> 00:06:03.863
And it keeps each ring's attack
surface reduction value from the run

98
00:06:03.863 --> 00:06:08.282
before the change, so putting it back
steps every moved rule back one stage.

99
00:06:08.282 --> 00:06:11.191
Applying it works like
publishing: a dry run,

100
00:06:11.191 --> 00:06:13.857
then apply with the plan's approved hash.

101
00:06:13.857 --> 00:06:17.145
The A S R values go back
into Intune by hand.

102
00:06:17.145 --> 00:06:21.858
And remember Microsoft's advice: an
exclusion is better than a step back.

103
00:06:21.858 --> 00:06:25.940
A rollback is for a broken
change, not one noisy rule.

104
00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:34.602
The go or no-go comes from the evidence,
with one command and the test stage.

105
00:06:34.602 --> 00:06:37.289
In fixture mode, after steps 1 to 3:

106
00:06:37.289 --> 00:06:41.665
every file matches, thirteen
records, chain unbroken.

107
00:06:41.665 --> 00:06:44.890
Pre-flight, publish and
the scheduled run pass.

108
00:06:44.890 --> 00:06:47.109
But access, coverage, prevention,

109
00:06:47.109 --> 00:06:51.465
A S R and detections were recorded
with their own checks failing.

110
00:06:51.465 --> 00:06:53.515
So it's no-go, as it should be.

111
00:06:53.515 --> 00:06:55.145
Go needs every row to pass:

112
00:06:55.145 --> 00:07:00.188
the test stage's findings closed and the
checks run again, the smoke test's alert,

113
00:07:00.188 --> 00:07:05.820
the rollback plan filed, and the security
officer's signature on the change record.

114
00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:11.395
Stage 2 is production.

115
00:07:11.395 --> 00:07:16.695
Its pre-flight adds a seventh check:
the test stage's go or no-go must pass.

116
00:07:16.695 --> 00:07:20.620
In fixture mode, it refuses,
naming the steps that failed.

117
00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:25.431
Once it passes, repeat steps 2 to 6 with
the production settings, one ring at

118
00:07:25.431 --> 00:07:30.320
a time, in the change board's window,
with the backout triggers the guide sets.

119
00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:33.830
The production go or
no-go also needs exposure,

120
00:07:33.830 --> 00:07:37.432
incidents, signals,
automation and the metrics.

121
00:07:37.432 --> 00:07:41.921
Finally, register the scheduled run
inside the boundary, every four weeks,

122
00:07:41.921 --> 00:07:45.820
through Privileged Identity
Management or a managed identity.

123
00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:50.500
The month's metrics, records and
hashes go to the security officer.

124
00:07:54.250 --> 00:07:58.062
Here's what the runbook's steps
support, against NIST's catalog.

125
00:07:58.062 --> 00:08:02.547
The pre-flight supports least privilege,
because the session holds only the

126
00:08:02.547 --> 00:08:07.175
needed scopes, and change control, because
the change is checked before it runs.

127
00:08:07.175 --> 00:08:10.075
The scheduled run supports
continuous monitoring.

128
00:08:10.075 --> 00:08:13.562
Publishing supports change
control and system monitoring.

129
00:08:13.562 --> 00:08:17.163
The rollback supports change
control and incident handling.

130
00:08:17.163 --> 00:08:22.300
And the go or no-go supports continuous
monitoring and protecting audit information.

131
00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:25.600
The indicators are FedRAMP's,
from the guide's rows.

132
00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:29.780
The records support these controls;
the assessor decides them.

133
00:08:33.530 --> 00:08:36.255
Last, the dangers and the checklist.

134
00:08:36.255 --> 00:08:41.155
Production before the test stage passes:
the seventh pre-flight check refuses it.

135
00:08:41.155 --> 00:08:45.080
A plan edited after approval:
the approved hash refuses it.

136
00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:49.543
A session in the wrong tenant: the
session check and the plan's tenant check.

137
00:08:49.543 --> 00:08:55.930
The detection API unavailable in GCC:
the portal path, from the same files.

138
00:08:55.930 --> 00:09:00.217
And a rollback written during the
outage: step 6 plans it before go.

139
00:09:00.217 --> 00:09:04.327
The checklist: both settings files
filed, the pre-flight recorded,

140
00:09:04.327 --> 00:09:08.588
the plan's hash approved, the smoke
test's alert, the rollback planned,

141
00:09:08.588 --> 00:09:12.544
every go or no-go row passing,
the production pre-flight passing,

142
00:09:12.544 --> 00:09:15.968
and the first monthly package
at the security officer.

143
00:09:15.968 --> 00:09:17.140
Thanks for watching.
